# Criteria (written before reading the diff)
C1 Soundness: no accepted program fails at run time (cast failure / missing service), at --opt 0 and --opt 2. In particular every `@main`/entry whose inferred environment contains an unprovided service is rejected (E0522), whatever spelling (annotated/unannotated, row variable + constructor applied to it, captured in closures, local closures used or unused).
C2 Principality: inferred scheme is most general; adding or removing a correct annotation does not change acceptance; base-accepted programs remain accepted with an equally general scheme (shown types for std/examples/bench identical or explainably equal).
C3 Bound removal is solution-preserving: a bound is removed only if it holds under every assignment (e.g. `N <: N | S(N)` by 4.5 rule 2), never when its removal loses a constraint (invariant constructor args, service args compared by equality - 4.5 rule 4; `N <: S(N)` is NOT redundant; `S(N) <: N` is not redundant).
C4 Genuinely recursive types still give E0505 (4.7 last bullet): x = x-in-itself via lists, functions, service args (`N = S(N)`), mutual recursion.
C5 9.2: one service constructor never appears with two different args in one environment (S(Int)|S(String) error; also through variables instantiated later; also after extrusion across levels).
C6 Level extrusion: value restriction / generalisation remains correct - no variable generalised that is still in the outer environment; service argument equality preserved across levels without making inner polymorphic functions monomorphic.
C7 Termination and time: solver terminates; no super-linear blowup vs base on deep helper chains / wide unions.
C8 Declaration-order independence: permuting top-level bindings changes neither acceptance nor types.
C9 Coalescing failures are reported as diagnostics everywhere (check, --types, interface, lowering) - never panic, never silently accepted; and no spurious failure for base-accepted programs.
C10 opt 0 and opt 2 behave identically for accepted programs.

